---

<DocsLayout
  title="Using the API"
  subtitle="Start a server and drive it — with curl and the typed client side by side, one task at a time."
  description="Quickstart guide for the nikcli HTTP API: starting a server, authenticating, selecting a project, creating sessions, prompting, reading diffs, files, search, shell commands and permissions."
  toc={toc}
>
  <section id="start" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Start a server</h2>
    <p>
      There is no hosted API. nikcli runs on your machine and the API is how everything else talks
      to it — the web app, the desktop app and the mobile app are all clients of these endpoints.
    </p>
    <DocCode language="bash">
nikcli serve --hostname 127.0.0.1 --port 4096
    </DocCode>
    <DocCallout type="warn" title="Read the port it prints">
      <p>
        If <code>4096</code> is taken the server falls back to an ephemeral port rather than
        failing. Take the URL from the output instead of hardcoding it.
      </p>
    </DocCallout>
  </section>

  <section id="first" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Your first request</h2>
    <p>Health is instance-less, so it answers before anything else is configured.</p>
    <DocCode language="bash">
curl -s http://127.0.0.1:4096/global/health
    </DocCode>
    <p>Then install the client, which is generated from the same contract this guide describes.</p>
    <DocCode language="bash">
npm install @nikcli-ai/sdk
    </DocCode>
    <DocCode language="ts">{`import { createNikcliClient } from "@nikcli-ai/sdk/httpapi"

const client = createNikcliClient({
  baseUrl: "http://127.0.0.1:4096",
  directory: process.cwd(),
})

const { data } = await client.global.health()
console.log(data)`}</DocCode>
    <DocCallout type="info" title="Calls resolve with { data, error }">
      <p>
        They do not reject by default. Guard on <code>data</code> — not on <code>error</code>,
        which is typed <code>unknown</code> and so does not narrow. Full explanation in
        <a href="/docs/build/client#results">the result envelope</a>.
      </p>
    </DocCallout>
  </section>

  <section id="auth" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Authenticate</h2>
    <p>
      On loopback with no password set, nothing is required. Once the server is reachable from
      elsewhere, it is.
    </p>
    <DocCode language="bash">
NIKCLI_SERVER_PASSWORD=secret nikcli serve --hostname 0.0.0.0 --port 4096

curl -s -u nikcli:secret http://127.0.0.1:4096/session
curl -s -H "Authorization: Bearer $NIKCLI_TOKEN" http://127.0.0.1:4096/session

# for clients that cannot set headers, such as EventSource
curl -s "http://127.0.0.1:4096/session?token=$NIKCLI_TOKEN"
    </DocCode>
    <DocCode language="ts">{`const client = createNikcliClient({
  baseUrl: "http://127.0.0.1:4096",
  headers: { Authorization: \`Bearer \${process.env.NIKCLI_TOKEN}\` },
})`}</DocCode>
  </section>

  <section id="directory" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Pick the project</h2>
    <p>
      One server hosts many projects, so most endpoints need to know which. Pass
      <code>directory</code> as a query parameter or as the <code>x-nikcli-directory</code> header —
      the client sets the header for you from its own <code>directory</code> option.
    </p>
    <DocCode language="bash">
curl -s "http://127.0.0.1:4096/project/current?directory=$PWD"
curl -s http://127.0.0.1:4096/project/current -H "x-nikcli-directory: $PWD"
    </DocCode>
    <DocCode language="ts">{`// the client default
await client.project.current()

// override for one call
await client.session.list({ directory: "/Users/me/other-project" })`}</DocCode>
  </section>

  <section id="prompt" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Create a session and prompt it</h2>
    <p>
      A session holds the conversation and the changes made in it. Create one, then send prompts to
      its id.
    </p>
    <DocCode language="bash" title="curl">{`SESSION=$(curl -s -X POST "http://127.0.0.1:4096/session?directory=$PWD" \\
  -H 'content-type: application/json' \\
  -d '{"title":"triage"}' | jq -r .id)

curl -s -X POST "http://127.0.0.1:4096/session/$SESSION/message?directory=$PWD" \\
  -H 'content-type: application/json' \\
  -d '{"parts":[{"type":"text","text":"Which tests are failing, and why?"}]}'
    `}</DocCode>
    <DocCode language="ts" title="SDK">{`const { data: session } = await client.session.create({ title: "triage" })

const { data: reply } = await client.session.prompt({
  sessionID: session!.id,
  parts: [{ type: "text", text: "Which tests are failing, and why?" }],
})

for (const part of reply!.parts) {
  if (part.type === "text") console.log(part.text)
}`}</DocCode>
    <p>
      <code>session.prompt</code> waits for the whole turn. To get output as it is produced, use
      <code>promptAsync</code> and the event feed — see
      <a href="/docs/guides/streaming">Streaming a response</a>.
    </p>
    <DocCallout type="note" title="parts, not a string">
      <p>
        A prompt is an array of parts, so one message can mix text with file attachments, an agent
        reference or a subtask. <code>{'{ type: "text", text }'}</code> is the common case.
      </p>
    </DocCallout>
  </section>

  <section id="diff" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Read what changed</h2>
    <p>
      Sessions edit files. Ask the session what it changed rather than diffing the working tree
      yourself — this scopes the answer to that session's work.
    </p>
    <DocCode language="bash">
curl -s "http://127.0.0.1:4096/session/$SESSION/diff?directory=$PWD"
curl -s "http://127.0.0.1:4096/session/$SESSION/message?directory=$PWD"
    </DocCode>
    <DocCode language="ts">{`const { data: diff } = await client.session.diff({ sessionID: session!.id })
const { data: messages } = await client.session.messages({ sessionID: session!.id, limit: 20 })`}</DocCode>
  </section>

  <section id="files" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Read and write files</h2>
    <p>
      File access goes through the instance, so paths are resolved inside the selected project
      rather than against the server's own working directory.
    </p>
    <DocCode language="bash">{`curl -s "http://127.0.0.1:4096/file/content?path=src/index.ts&directory=$PWD"

curl -s -X PUT "http://127.0.0.1:4096/file/content?directory=$PWD" \\
  -H 'content-type: application/json' \\
  -d '{"path":"NOTES.md","content":"# Notes\\n"}'

curl -s "http://127.0.0.1:4096/file/status?directory=$PWD"
    `}</DocCode>
    <DocCode language="ts">{`const { data: file } = await client.file.read({ path: "src/index.ts" })
await client.file.write({ path: "NOTES.md", content: "# Notes\\n" })
const { data: status } = await client.file.status()`}</DocCode>
  </section>

  <section id="search" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Search the project</h2>
    <p>Three searches, matching what the agent's own tools use: text, filenames and symbols.</p>
    <DocCode language="bash">
curl -s "http://127.0.0.1:4096/find?pattern=TODO&directory=$PWD"
curl -s "http://127.0.0.1:4096/find/file?query=router&directory=$PWD"
curl -s "http://127.0.0.1:4096/find/symbol?query=createNikcliClient&directory=$PWD"
    </DocCode>
    <DocCode language="ts">{`const { data: matches } = await client.find.text({ pattern: "TODO" })
const { data: files } = await client.find.files({ query: "router" })
const { data: symbols } = await client.find.symbols({ query: "createNikcliClient" })`}</DocCode>
  </section>

  <section id="shell" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Run a shell command</h2>
    <p>
      Running a command <em>inside</em> a session records it in the transcript, so the agent sees
      the result on the next turn. That is usually what you want over shelling out yourself.
    </p>
    <DocCode language="ts">{`const { data: result } = await client.session.shell({
  sessionID: session!.id,
  agent: "build",
  command: "bun test",
})`}</DocCode>
    <DocCallout type="warn" title="agent is required here">
      <p>
        <code>session.shell</code> takes an <code>agent</code>, because the command runs under that
        agent's permissions rather than unconditionally.
      </p>
    </DocCallout>
  </section>

  <section id="permissions" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Answer a permission request</h2>
    <p>
      If the agent asks for approval and nothing answers, the turn waits. An automated client has to
      decide — which is exactly why the endpoints exist.
    </p>
    <DocCode language="ts">{`const { data: pending } = await client.permission.list()

for (const request of pending ?? []) {
  await client.permission.reply({
    requestID: request.id,
    reply: "once", // "once" | "always" | "reject"
  })
}`}</DocCode>
    <p>
      Watching <code>permission.asked</code> on the event feed is the reactive version of the same
      thing, and is how the TUI and the mobile app do it.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="script" class="doc-section">
    <h2>A complete script</h2>
    <p>
      Putting it together: open a session, ask it to do something, approve whatever it asks for, and
      print the resulting diff.
    </p>
    <DocCode language="ts" title="review.ts">{`import { createNikcliClient } from "@nikcli-ai/sdk/httpapi"

const client = createNikcliClient({
  baseUrl: process.env.NIKCLI_URL ?? "http://127.0.0.1:4096",
  directory: process.cwd(),
  headers: process.env.NIKCLI_TOKEN
    ? { Authorization: \`Bearer \${process.env.NIKCLI_TOKEN}\` }
    : undefined,
})

const { data: session, error } = await client.session.create({ title: "automated review" })
if (!session) throw error

// approve anything this session asks for while it works
const approve = setInterval(async () => {
  const { data: pending } = await client.permission.list()
  for (const request of pending ?? []) {
    await client.permission.reply({ requestID: request.id, reply: "once" })
  }
}, 1000)

try {
  const { data: reply } = await client.session.prompt({
    sessionID: session.id,
    parts: [{ type: "text", text: "Review the staged changes and fix anything obviously broken." }],
  })

  for (const part of reply?.parts ?? []) {
    if (part.type === "text") console.log(part.text)
  }

  const { data: diff } = await client.session.diff({ sessionID: session.id })
  console.log(diff)
} finally {
  clearInterval(approve)
}`}</DocCode>
    <DocCallout type="warn" title="Approving everything is a choice, not a default">
      <p>
        The loop above says yes to every request. That is reasonable in a sandbox or a throwaway CI
        container, and not reasonable on a machine that holds credentials. Filter on
        <code>request.permission</code> if the script runs anywhere real.
      </p>
    </DocCallout>
  </section>

